      "answer": "According to the knowledge base, SnowflakeNet [5] is a Transformer-based method that still adopts explicit kNNs in its architecture [S1][S2]. In contrast, PointAttN is designed to eliminate kNNs entirely by properly designing a framework that fully exploits the advantages of self-attention and cross-attention mechanisms [S1]. Specifically, PointAttN introduces a geometric details perception (GDP) block that leverages cross-attention to adaptively aggregate information from unordered points, implicitly modeling local features without relying on kNN-based local partitions [S1]. This kNN-free design eliminates the limited receptive field of explicit local partition, allowing PointAttN to better perceive both local details and global structures [S2]. As a result, PointAttN achieves more precise geometric structures and local details than SnowflakeNet, as shown in categories such as plane, car, and lamp [S2]. Quantitatively, PointAttN achieves a 4.9% reduction in average CD compared with SnowflakeNet (6.86 versus 7.21) on the PCN dataset [S4].",
